Skip the idle process in find_pid_by_name on Windows.
The list from find_pid_by_name ends at a 0 entry, but on Windows PID 0
is a real process, the idle process "[System Process]". As it comes
first in the process list, an operand that matched it ended the list
before any other match: pidof printed nothing and failed, and killall
reported "no process killed", even while another process of that name
was running, such as "[System Process].exe", which matches in its first
15 bytes. Had PID 0 been passed on, killall would have called kill(0),
which signals the caller and its descendants.

Leave PID 0 out of the list. It cannot be signalled anyway.

---
 libbb/find_pid_by_name.c |    6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

--- a/libbb/find_pid_by_name.c
+++ b/libbb/find_pid_by_name.c
@@ -90,6 +90,12 @@
 
 	pidList = xzalloc(sizeof(*pidList));
 	while ((p = procps_scan(p, PSSCAN_PID|PSSCAN_COMM|PSSCAN_ARGVN|PSSCAN_EXE))) {
+#if ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
+		/* Skip the idle process: its PID of 0 would end the list,
+		 * and kill(0) would signal the caller instead */
+		if (p->pid == 0)
+			continue;
+#endif
 		if (comm_match(p, procName)
 #if !ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
 		/* or we require argv0 to match (essential for matching reexeced /proc/self/exe)*/
